#ifndef VERSION_MBM_H
#define VERSION_MBM_H

#ifndef MBM_VERSION
/*
    Format: "X.Y" or "X.Y.Z"
    X - Major change
    Y - New functionalities
    Z - Fix some issues or even improved non impact of new functionalities (optional patch component)

    Historical entries below predate the "Z" patch component and used a trailing
    letter (e.g. "3.1.a") instead; new entries should prefer numeric "X.Y.Z".

    1.0.a Framework began using Directx
    1.2.a Framework changed to Opengl-ES
    1.3.a Added wrapper Java for Android
    1.9.e Removed Directx
    2.0.a Added LUA as Language script
    2.1.a Added Editor LUA (sprite maker, scene2dw,scene2ds,scene3d)
    2.2.a Added Editor LUA (particle editor)
    2.3.a Added Editor LUA (font editor)
    2.4.a Changed internal way of binary mesh (introduced version as define)
    2.5.x Added TMX binary file
    2.6.x General re work
    2.7.x Included CMake
    2.8.a Make shared library of core-mbm
    3.0.a Plugins introduced (ImGui, box2d) as Modules
    3.1.a Sprite maker editor based on ImGui plugin added
    3.1.b Improved / fix Sprite maker editor based on ImGui
    3.1.c Created Shader editor based on ImGui / fix Minor issue on Shader Lua
    3.1.d Created Scene 2d Editor / removed old scenes editor and resources
    3.1.e Scene 2d Editor added isRelative2ds option
    3.1.f Updated sqlite3 to version 3.24.0. (fixed issue: ERROR attempt to index a string value) replaced luaL_register by luaL_newlib.
    3.2.a Added Tile Map from own engine. Removed TMX support
    3.2.b Added texture packer editor
    3.2.c Fixed endFX issue
    3.2.e Update animation even when is not in the screen
    3.2.f Fixed issue for vertex shader (when set to null it was not freeing )
    3.2.g Added compatibility to load Triangle from version 3
    3.2.f Parser for txt on font created
    3.2.g Upgrade IM-GUI version to 1.78, tinyfiledialog (v3.6.3)
    3.3   mini-mbm on windows does not depend anymore on core_mbm
    3.4   refactoried files on LUA wrapper
    3.5   Frozen box 2d (2.3.2)
    3.6   Migrated to box2d (2.4.1)
    4.0   LiquidFun for box 2d, this version forces to drawback Box2D to 2.3.0.
    4.1   LiquidFun for box 2d 2.3.0 and box 2d 2.4.1 in library separated
    4.2   Reorganized framework, moved needed function to plugins, LiquidFun and Box2d Available.
    5.0   Include Directx 9 backend support. Refactoried core-manager-opengl_es to core-manager-renderer with support to Directx9 and Opengl-ES and future renderers.
    5.1   Updated IM-GUI version to 1.92.6
    5.2   Introduced MacOs support
    5.3   Modernized Android Project, Added native Activity,removed old Java wrapper for AUDIO jni, added support to generate project on Android Studio using cmake
    6.0   The first Mini MBM lighting implementation using the classic model of Phong.
    6.1   TextureAnimationEffect ownership normalized to animation-level metadata with explicit legacy mismatch validation.
    6.2   Mesh v10 stores TextureAnimationEffect once per animation FX block instead of duplicating it in PS/VS stage records.
    6.3   Mesh v11 stores TextureAnimationEffect once per animation FX block instead of duplicating it in PS/VS stage records.
    6.4   Lighting system created with support multiple light sources and improved performance. Mesh v12 introduces a new lighting.
    6.5.0 Added reusable Blender-style 3D orbit navigation gizmo (editor_utils.lua drawOrbitGizmo) to mesh_debug's Camera window; split the Light panel into its own window. First entry using the X.Y.Z format.
    6.6.0 Added Scene Editor 3D (editor/scene_editor3d.lua): grid/free placement of any renderizable, per-layer Y control, cached mesh thumbnails via render2texture, async mesh loading with progress, and a self-contained requirable scene export.
    6.6.1 Fixed Scene Editor 3D: Combo() off-by-one index bug (Map type/snap-scale combos), ColorEdit4 crash in the light panel (wrong calling convention + wrong 0-255 color range), background color menu parity with Scene Editor 2D, thumbnail cache location, per-tab orbit camera, sync reload of already-cached meshes, and scroll-into-scene leak while hovering ImGui windows.
    6.6.2 Added tImGui.GetWantCaptureMouse()/GetWantCaptureKeyboard() (plugins/imGui): expose io.WantCaptureMouse/WantCaptureKeyboard for reading, the correct/documented way to decide whether input should reach the game scene vs the UI, replacing IsAnyWindowHovered() which repeatedly let clicks/scroll on Scene Editor 3D's own windows leak into the 3D scene. Also fixed the editor's stray white render-to-texture quad, mislabeled/too-short camera zoom, and made Layer-tab placement synchronous.
    6.7.0 Physic Editor: fixed a crash loading 3D meshes (selection/highlight API was 2D-only); added a real 3D orbit camera + nav gizmo, mouse-based selection of physics shapes via native :collide(), and full editing support for all physics types on 3D meshes. Added a "Complex" primitive (8-point box, plus a 12-triangle decomposition) to the Add-Physic UI. Mesh Debug: added a bulk "Reset Physics To" operation in Apply-to-all, computing each mesh's own frame-1 vertex bounds instead of assuming mesh:getSize().
    6.8.0 Added a real ray/AABB test for 3D screen-space picking: DEVICE::rayIntersectsAABB (slab method) plus a new mbm.getPickRay(sx,sy) Lua binding exposing DEVICE::rayCast (origin + normalized direction), previously only used internally to back mbm.to3d. Fixed obj:collide(x,y) on 3D objects to use this instead of unprojecting at a guessed depth (the object's own raw world Z, treated as camera distance) -- that approximation degenerated to the camera's own position for any object near world Z=0 (e.g. anything at its default placement position), so the test could never register a hit there; it also mattered generally for any free-orbiting camera since an object's Z has no fixed relationship to its real camera distance. Also fixed obj:collide(x, y, useAABB) (the 4-argument form): onCheckCollisionBoundingBoxRenderizable only accepted an argument count of exactly 3, so passing the documented optional 4th useAABB argument missed that branch entirely and hit a generic "Expected: [renderizable],[renderizable] or [x,y]" error instead. Discovered and fixed while building Scene Editor 3D's Shift+drag rectangle-select. Added Scene Editor 3D: grid show/hide toggle, Free-mode grid bordering (snapping still skipped), live mesh-offset propagation to preview/placed instances, Main Scene/Mesh property tab renames, replace-not-duplicate placement on occupied grid cells, and a full select/rotate/delete workflow (multi-select, Shift+drag rectangle select, Esc to unselect, bottom-right rotate/delete tool window) mirroring tilemap_editor.lua.
    6.9.0 Added a true AABB-center concept, engine-wide: RENDERIZABLE::getBoundingAABBCenterOffset() (new, Impl-backed, virtual) reports the world-space offset from an object's pivot to its actual geometric center -- (0,0,0) when geometry is centered on its own pivot (the common case), non-zero for anything anchored elsewhere (a mesh pivoted at its floor/base, a character at its feet). Backed by a new INFO_PHYSICS::getBoundsMinMax() (both 2D/3D), which also properly unions bounds across every physics shape kind on a mesh, unlike getBounds(). New Lua binding obj:getAABBCenter(). TEXT_DRAW overrides the offset directly from its own alignment-driven aabbMin/aabbMax. Replaced the old doOffsetIfText()/undoOffsetIfText() mutate-position-then-restore hack (font-only, used inside obj:collide) and editor_utils.lua's independently-written font-only correction in setShapeToMesh with this one general mechanism -- obj:collide and the shared 2D editor selection-highlight shape now both use it uniformly. Also fixed INFO_PHYSICS::getBounds()'s CUBE/SPHERE branches (both 2D/3D overloads), which derived size via 2*max(|absCenter-halfDim|,|absCenter+halfDim|) -- only correct when absCenter==(0,0,0); for an off-center shape (e.g. the single CUBE MESH_MBM_DEBUG::fillAtLeastOneBound() auto-bakes into any mesh saved with no hand-authored physics, whose absCenter is the mesh's own true center) this could inflate the reported size up to 2x on the anchored axis. Physic Editor: new physics shapes now default their position to the mesh's true AABB center instead of the hardcoded local origin, and a small marker (distinct from the existing hover-highlight point) shows that center while editing. Scene Editor 3D: hover wire-box and Mesh Set thumbnail camera focus now use the true center instead of the pivot. Root-caused while investigating a Scene Editor 3D hover-box bug reported against a floor-anchored test building mesh.
    6.10.0 Scene Editor 3D (Map edition tab): replaced the plain yellow wireframe hover box with a solid translucent shape (green when the hovered/selected mesh belongs to the active layer, red otherwise; selected meshes blink between alpha 0.2-0.5, a per-frame sine wave, no shader needed), mirroring tilemap_editor.lua's/physic_editor.lua's own tinted-overlay visual language instead of a bespoke wireframe. Added Ctrl+Click to toggle one mesh into/out of the current selection (additive), complementing the existing Shift+drag rectangle-select (which replaces). Placed Meshes list rows and the active layer's "(active)" label now render in green to match. Mesh property tab: added per-asset Rotation and Scale offsets (added on top of each instance's own rotation/scale, same as the existing Position offset), applied consistently in the live preview, every placed instance, and the exported game script's runtime loader. Map tab: directional light direction is now edited via the same orbit trackball widget already used for the camera (tUtil.drawOrbitGizmo, reused unmodified since it only touches .azimuth/.elevation) instead of three raw x/y/z InputFloats, plus a visual arrow in the 3D scene showing where the light comes from and a small tinted marker at each point light's position -- there was previously no way to see where a light actually was/pointed relative to the placed scene, only numbers in a panel.
    6.10.1 Fixed MESH_MBM_DEBUG::getInfo(filePath, ...) (the lightweight file-only info query used by Mesh Debug's UI, e.g. the Normals/Light 3D panels) never reading each mesh's actual hasNormal/hasUv flags from its SECTION_FRAME_STATIC payload -- it only counted frames, leaving hasNorText at its struct-default (normals always false), so freshly-saved normals appeared to vanish from the UI even though MESH_MBM_DEBUG::loadV11 (the real load path used for rendering) already parsed them correctly and the file was fine on disk. Now parses frame 0's FRAME_HEADER_V11 the same way loadV11 does.
    6.11.0 Fixed two reserved-lighting-shader bugs affecting every 3D lit mesh, in all three render backends (OpenGL ES, DirectX9, Metal): (1) Directional light appeared to change with camera orbit. Root cause: the per-object matrix uploaded as the vertex shader's "mvMatrix" uniform (which builds vNormalView/vPositionView) was SHADER::modelView -- the model matrix only, never multiplied by the camera's view matrix -- so despite the name, those varyings were actually world-space, not view-space, while getLightDirectionView()/getLightPositionView()/getPointLightPositionView() already correctly rotated (and, for position, translated) light data into true view space via camera.matrixView/matrixView2d. Dotting a world-space normal against a view-space light direction is what made lighting swing with camera orbit. Fixed by adding a second static, SHADER::mvMatrixLightSpace (modelView x camera view, computed by the new SHADER::updateMvpAndLightMatrices() helper alongside the existing mvpMatrix), and uploading that instead of modelView for "mvMatrix" -- SHADER::modelView itself, and the mvpMatrix path, are untouched. This also fixes a latent, previously-unreported 2D bug: point-light attenuation was already silently wrong any time a 2D world camera panned away from the origin, for the same world/view-space mismatch reason. (2) Point lights had no visible effect on 3D meshes. Root cause: getReservedLightMode() hardcoded 3D to directional-only (mode 1) or off (mode 0) -- the point-light per-pixel array path (mode 2) was only ever selected for 2D world sprites, even though mbm.addPointLight('3d', ...) correctly stored the light and DEVICE's per-object point-light selection was already fully LIGHT_TARGET-generic. Fixed by making 3D's mode 1 run the directional term AND a point-light loop together (a new DirectionalColor uniform keeps the two from clobbering the same LightColor[0] slot), instead of introducing a third mode -- so a 3D mesh can now be lit by its directional light and any nearby point lights at once, matching how a real scene's lighting composes. 2D's existing point-only behavior (mode 2) is untouched.
    6.12.0 Mesh Debug: the Normals tree node now visualizes frame-1's per-vertex normals as lines whenever it's open (any type with normals -- sprite/tile/mesh/etc, not just 'mesh' -- drawn in 2dw or 3d space to match the current camera mode), color-coded green/red by comparing each stored normal against the geometric face normal derived from triangle winding (meshD:getIndex/getVertex), so a wrong-facing normal is obvious without guessing. The geometric-normal computation accounts for meshD:getModeFrontFace() (mini-mbm's own mesh default is CW, not the textbook CCW-is-outward convention), otherwise every correctly-wound mesh would be reported as universally flipped -- an earlier iteration of this also called the C++-internal method name (getModeFrontFaceDirection) instead of the actual Lua-bound one (getModeFrontFace), which just no-ops via this file's pcall wrapper and silently left every normal miscategorized. Added a per-subset vertex table (nx/ny/nz DragFloats, OK/Flipped status, per-row and per-subset-bulk Flip and Recompute-from-geometry actions, kept to 5 narrow columns plus a table-local horizontal scrollbar so it fits the Mesh Tree window's default width) so individual normals can be fixed inline instead of only globally via Add/Remove Normals. Correctness coloring and Recompute are skipped (with a note) when the mesh's draw mode isn't TRIANGLES, since face winding isn't well-defined there; Flip still works. Also fixed the pre-existing Camera/Light 3D panels (showCameraWindow/showLightWindow) rendering off the right edge of the screen on macOS: their right-anchored SetNextWindowPos math used mbm.getSizeScreen() (logical/scaled points) instead of mbm.getRealSizeScreen() (physical framebuffer pixels, the space SetNextWindowPos actually operates in), which only diverges on HiDPI/Retina displays. Fixed a normal-viz line leak: showMeshTreeWindow's per-mesh tree node only calls showMeshOptions (which owned the Normals node's auto-cancel-on-close) for whichever entry is currently expanded/selected, so switching to a different loaded mesh left the previous entry's normal lines destroy-less and permanently visible, stacking up across every mesh visited; the outer loop's collapsed-row branch now destroys them unconditionally instead of relying on showMeshOptions running. Applied the same fix to the pre-existing, identically-shaped leak in the Transform tab's preview clone (tXformPreviewMesh). Purely an editor/ *.lua addition/fix, no engine/Lua-binding changes.
    6.13.0 Two follow-up fixes to 6.11.0's lighting round, both found by testing real scenes (game-template/scenes/light-multi-test.lua and a floor mesh in Scene Editor 3D) rather than by inspection: (1) 2D point lights on a sprite with no normal map (HasNormalMap == 0) regressed from a soft glow to a tiny, sharp, geometry-free hotspot. Root cause: that path still ran the real diffuse dot-product (max(dot(normalView, lightDir), 0)) against a synthetic flat normalView=(0,0,1), and 6.11.0's mvMatrix fix removed an old, accidental, asymmetric Z-offset between light and fragment position that used to make that dot product forgiving almost everywhere -- once symmetric (correct), the true (tiny) Z-gap between an in-plane light and an in-plane sprite collapsed the dot product to a narrow hotspot. Fixed by skipping the angle term entirely for this case (diffuse = 1.0 when HasNormalMap == 0) -- distance-only falloff, matching how 2D point lights work in most other engines. Sprites with a real bound normal map (HasNormalMap != 0, e.g. src/test-lib/box.spt + wooden-box_normal.png) are unaffected: they keep the real per-pixel dot product, which is supposed to be sharp/angle-sensitive since that's the entire point of a normal map. (2) Any 3D mesh with lighting enabled and zero point lights configured was silently picking up a phantom point light. Root cause: selectPointLightsForObjectImpl()'s pre-existing fallback to LIGHT_STATE's single legacy point-light slot (for games that only ever called mbm.setPointLight, never addPointLight) fired whenever the point-light list was empty, with no check for whether that legacy slot had ever actually been configured -- so it silently exposed LIGHT_STATE's bare struct defaults (pointPosition=(0,0,128), radius=512, white) as a fake selected light. Harmless while 3D never consumed point-light selection at all; very much not once 6.11.0 made it start to. Fixed by gating that fallback on lightState.pointPositionConfigured/pointColorConfigured/pointRadiusConfigured (added specifically for this purpose) -- the fallback now only fires for targets where a legacy setter was actually called at least once. docs/light.md gained a beginner-level "Quick Mental Model" section (the three lighting terms, where the Normal comes from in each of the three common cases, the 2D light-height/Z convention, and a diagnostic checklist) since both of these were found by a user working through exactly that reasoning by hand.
    6.14.0 Two MESH_MBM_DEBUG bugs found while building a "known-good" reference mesh to test normals/lighting against (src/core_mbm/mesh-manager.cpp): (1) calculateNormals() (the auto-recompute-from-geometry path behind meshDebug:addNormals()) used the wrong SUBSET_DEBUG fields for its indexed-mesh loop bound (vertexStart/vertexCount instead of indexStart/indexCount) and had an off-by-one loop condition (strict '<' instead of '<=') in both the indexed and non-indexed branches -- together these meant auto-normal generation silently produced all-zero normals for the overwhelmingly common case (vertex count != index count), and always skipped each subset's last triangle even when it did run. (2) addIndex() -- the from-scratch mesh-building API used by meshDebug:addIndex() -- applied its vertexStart offset correction to the wrong slice of the index buffer when appending a new subset's indices (it read the not-yet-updated pSubset->indexStart instead of the already-correct indexCountBefore computed earlier in the same call), silently corrupting every previously-added subset's stored indices each time a further subset's indices were added; a related wrong-source-offset bug in the same function's index-buffer memmove (copying "after" indices from oldIndex[0] instead of oldIndex[indexCountBefore+oldSizeIndex]) was fixed alongside it. Both bugs are latent in the from-scratch mesh-authoring path used by any tool that builds a multi-subset mesh one subset at a time (the ordinary, documented way) -- verified via a controlled reproduction (4-vertex/6-index quad, then a 24-vertex/6-subset cube) that came back with garbage/zeroed indices and normals before the fix and correct ones after. Mesh Debug: added an "Add Colored Cube" menu item (right below "Load from folder") that builds a 24-vertex, 6-subset reference cube in Lua via the same from-scratch meshDebug API, one solid hex-texture color per face (no shared vertices, so addNormals() can't smooth-average across faces), saves it to src/test-lib/colored_cube.msh, and loads it into the tree like any other mesh -- a repeatable, trustworthy reference/orientation asset now that the two bugs above no longer stand in the way. Two more issues surfaced by actually testing the generated cube: (3) MESH::render() (src/render/mesh.cpp) draws nothing at all for a mesh with zero animations, even a static single-frame one, since it looks up the current animation before doing any drawing and bails out if there isn't one -- the cube builder now calls meshDebug:addAnim() to add a trivial frame-1-to-1 animation so the cube (and any similarly-built from-scratch mesh) renders immediately. (4) The cube visually rendered inside-out (its true outward faces culled away, showing the inside instead) with setModeFrontFace('CCW'), despite CCW being the mathematically correct description of its winding under calculateNormals()'s uncorrected cross product -- this engine's actual screen-space rasterization treats that same winding as CW-front, matching the already-documented fact that mini-mbm's own mesh convention is CW, not the textbook CCW-is-outward one. Fixed by setting 'CW' instead; normals and culling are independent mechanisms here so this doesn't affect the (separately verified) per-face normals. Also replaced Mesh Debug's raw X/Y/Z directional-light sliders with the same orbit-trackball gizmo already used for the camera and for Scene Editor 3D's own light direction; the azimuth/elevation<->direction-vector conversion (previously duplicated in scene_editor3d.lua) is now shared via editor_utils.lua's tUtil.dirFromOrbit/tUtil.orbitFromDir. Also fixed mesh_debug.lua's Light panel (getPreviewStage2Texture) and Shader tree node spamming "[mesh_debug]... type of class [1][mesh] not implemented for FX!!!" to the console every frame whenever the previewed mesh has no active shader animation (e.g. any static mesh like this new cube) -- MESH::getFx() legitimately returns null in that case, an expected/frequent state, not a bug worth logging on every single frame; switched from the print-on-failure dpCall wrapper to a plain pcall for that specific probe.
    6.15.0 Fixed a real false-positive/false-negative bug in Mesh Debug's Normals tree node OK/Flipped check (editor/mesh_debug.lua, computeGeoNormalsForSubset), found while cross-checking the new colored cube (6.14.0) against base.msh. The check compared each stored normal against a geometric cross(p1-p0, p2-p0) that it sign-flipped whenever the mesh's declared front-face wasn't CCW, on the assumption that CW-declared meshes have their raw cross product pointing inward -- but that assumption doesn't hold in general: culling (glFrontFace, screen-space triangle winding) and lighting (the stored per-vertex normal) are independent mechanisms with no fixed relationship, and whether a mesh's raw cross product happens to point outward or inward depends on how its vertices were actually wound, not on its front-face string. Confirmed both ways: the hand-built colored cube (front_face=CW, raw cross genuinely outward, verified correct by actual rendering) was misreported as universally "Flipped"; base.msh (a Blender export, also front_face=CW) was misreported as universally "OK" despite its top and bottom faces having genuinely swapped normals -- confirmed independently by classifying vertices via actual Y position rather than trusting their stored normal, and by a live light test (an overhead light lit the bottom face instead of the top). The old heuristic wasn't just imprecise, it was mathematically guaranteed to rubber-stamp that exact swapped-normal defect as correct. Fixed by always using the same unconditional raw cross product MESH_MBM_DEBUG::calculateNormals() itself uses (no front-face sign flip), so the check answers its one real question -- does the stored normal agree with what this engine's own "Recompute from geometry" would produce -- consistently. User-verified after the fix: recomputing base.msh's normals now correctly resolves its swapped top/bottom (status OK, lit from the expected side), and the colored cube's normals read OK as expected. Also added a read-only X/Y/Z direction readout (one line per axis, to avoid overflowing the panel width) below the directional-light orbit gizmo in both Mesh Debug and Scene Editor 3D, so the gizmo's current direction is visible as numbers, not just a trackball position.
*/
#define MBM_VERSION "6.15.0" // MBM_VERSION must be in format X.Y or X.Y.Z
#endif

#endif
